issues.body:
# Environment

Knex version: 3.3.0
Database + version: SQLite 3.x through better-sqlite3 13.0.3 (WAL mode)
OS: Linux (Node 26.10.0)

# Bug

1. When another connection or process holds the write lock, a transaction that reads and then writes fails at its first write with `SQLITE_BUSY`, even though `busy_timeout` is set. I'd expect it to wait for the lock, as single statements outside a transaction do.

   Knex starts every SQLite transaction with `BEGIN;` (`lib/dialects/sqlite3/execution/sqlite-transaction.js`), which is a DEFERRED transaction. If its first statement is a SELECT, it holds a read transaction, and its first write must upgrade it, which fails with `SQLITE_BUSY` when another connection is writing (https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html#deferred_immediate_and_exclusive_transactions). The busy handler doesn't help here: SQLite skips it when waiting could deadlock, as when a read lock is being promoted (https://www.sqlite.org/c3ref/busy_handler.html). `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` takes the write lock up front, where `busy_timeout` does apply.

   In practice the other writer is often not your own code: Litestream replicating the database, a CLI or migration running next to the server, or a queue worker in a second process.

2. Error message

   ```
   SQLITE_BUSY insert into `requests` (`days`) values (2) - database is locked
   ```

3. Reduced test code (runs with `npm install knex@3.3.0 better-sqlite3`):

   ```js
   // Knex + better-sqlite3: a transaction that reads, then writes, fails with SQLITE_BUSY while
   // another connection holds the write lock, even with busy_timeout set. Knex always sends
   // `BEGIN;` (DEFERRED), and once a deferred transaction has read, SQLite cannot make its first
   // write wait for the lock: https://www.sqlite.org/lang_transaction.html#deferred_immediate_and_exclusive_transactions
   const fs = require('node:fs');
   const os = require('node:os');
   const path = require('node:path');
   const { Worker } = require('node:worker_threads');

   const dir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'knex-busy-'));
   const filename = path.join(dir, 'test.sqlite3');

   const knex = require('knex')({
     client: 'better-sqlite3',
     connection: { filename },
     useNullAsDefault: true,
     pool: {
       afterCreate(conn, done) {
         conn.pragma('journal_mode = WAL');
         conn.pragma('busy_timeout = 5000');
         done();
       },
     },
   });

   async function main() {
     await knex.schema.createTable('requests', (t) => {
       t.increments('id');
       t.integer('days');
     });

     // Another writer (in production: Litestream, a CLI command, a second process) holds the
     // write lock for 100 ms.
     const writer = new Worker(
       `
       const { parentPort, workerData } = require('node:worker_threads');
       const db = new (require('better-sqlite3'))(workerData.filename);
       db.exec('BEGIN IMMEDIATE');
       db.prepare('INSERT INTO requests (days) VALUES (1)').run();
       parentPort.postMessage('locked');
       Atomics.wait(new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4)), 0, 0, 100);
       db.exec('COMMIT');
       db.close();
       `,
       { eval: true, workerData: { filename } }
     );
     await new Promise((resolve) => writer.once('message', resolve));

     try {
       await knex.transaction(async (trx) => {
         const [{ count }] = await trx('requests').count({ count: '*' });
         await trx('requests').insert({ days: Number(count) + 2 });
       });
       console.log('ok: the transaction waited for the lock');
     } catch (error) {
       console.log(`failed: ${error.code} ${error.message}`);
     } finally {
       await new Promise((resolve) => writer.once('exit', resolve));
       await knex.destroy();
       fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
     }
   }

   main();
   ```

   Output: `failed: SQLITE_BUSY insert into requests ... - database is locked`. Starting the transaction with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` instead makes it wait ~100 ms and succeed.

# Feature discussion / request

1. Use case: SQLite in production with WAL, where a second writer exists (Litestream, a separate worker process, CLI commands). In a benchmark of 20 read-then-write transactions per second with Litestream running, about 10 in 1,390 failed this way; with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, none did.

2. Knex has no way to choose the SQLite transaction behavior today. `isolationLevel` and `readOnly` are accepted but only log a warning on SQLite.

3. API proposal, opt-in so nothing changes for existing users. Either or both of:

   - per transaction, similar to `enforceForeignCheck` from #6315:
     ```js
     await knex.transaction(async (trx) => { ... }, { behavior: 'immediate' }) // 'deferred' | 'immediate' | 'exclusive'
     ```
   - a client-level default for the sqlite3 and better-sqlite3 dialects, with read-only transactions staying deferred:
     ```js
     require('knex')({ client: 'better-sqlite3', connection: { ... }, transactionBehavior: 'immediate' })
     ```

   Other libraries made the same move: Rails 8 uses IMMEDIATE by default for SQLite (rails/rails#50371), Laravel added a `transaction_mode` option (laravel/framework#56681), and Drizzle has a per-transaction `behavior` option.

I'm happy to open a PR with integration tests and docs once you've said which API you'd prefer.

# Environment

Knex version: 3.3.0
Database + version: any
OS: any
TypeScript definitions related — tagging @lorefnon

# Feature discussion / request

## 1. Explain what is your use case

I need a runtime type guard that tells me whether an arbitrary value is a knex query (either a `QueryBuilder` or a `Raw`), so I can branch on it before passing it along:

```ts
import type { Knex } from 'knex';

export function isKnexQuery(value: unknown): value is Knex.Raw | Knex.QueryBuilder {
  return value instanceof KnexBuilder || value instanceof KnexRaw;
}
```

The only way to get the constructors today is a deep import into the package internals:

```ts
import KnexBuilder from 'knex/lib/query/querybuilder';
import KnexRaw from 'knex/lib/raw';
```

which TypeScript rejects, because those internal files ship no declarations:

```
Could not find a declaration file for module 'knex/lib/query/querybuilder'. 'node_modules/knex/lib/query/querybuilder.js' implicitly has an 'any' type.
  If the 'knex' package actually exposes this module, try adding a new declaration (.d.ts) file containing `declare module 'knex/lib/query/querybuilder';`
```

The suggested workaround (a hand-written ambient `declare module` block) is not something I want in my codebase: it types the imports as `any`, it silently keeps compiling if the internal path is moved or renamed in a future knex release, and ambient module declarations leak into every consumer of my package's own types.

Note that this is specifically about the *runtime classes*. `Knex.QueryBuilder` and `Knex.Raw` already exist in `types/index.d.ts`, but they are type-only interfaces, so `value instanceof Knex.Raw` is not expressible.

For `Raw` there is an undocumented escape hatch — `Raw.prototype.isRawInstance = true` (`lib/raw.js`), which knex itself relies on internally (`lib/client.js`, `lib/formatter/wrappingFormatter.js`, `lib/query/querybuilder.js`, …) — but it is not part of the public API, it is not in the type definitions, and there is no equivalent marker for `QueryBuilder` at all.

There is a related discussion in #6269 about replacing `isRawInstance` with a `Symbol.for()`-based check, and a similar request for a different internal class in #4999 (expose `FsMigrations`). This request is narrower: make the two most commonly needed classes reachable from the main entry point.

## 2. Explain what kind of feature would support this

Expose the actual `QueryBuilder` and `Raw` constructors from the package root, and type them in `types/index.d.ts`, so that `instanceof` works without reaching into `knex/lib/*`.

Today `lib/knex-builder/Knex.js` already attaches `Client` as a real class:

```js
knex.Client = Client;
```

but `knex.QueryBuilder` is deliberately *not* the class — it is a wrapper object that only carries `extend`:

```js
knex.QueryBuilder = {
  extend: function (methodName, fn) {
    QueryBuilder.extend(methodName, fn);
    QueryInterface.push(methodName);
  },
};
```

and `Raw` is not exposed at all.

## 3. Give some API proposal, how the feature should work

Because `knex.QueryBuilder` is already taken by the `extend`-only object, replacing it outright would be a breaking change for anyone calling `knex.QueryBuilder.extend(...)`. Two options, in order of preference:

**Option A — additive, non-breaking.** Export the constructors under distinct names:

```js
// lib/knex-builder/Knex.js
knex.QueryBuilderClass = require('../query/querybuilder');
knex.Raw = require('../raw');
```

```ts
// types/index.d.ts
export const QueryBuilderClass: new (...args: any[]) => Knex.QueryBuilder;
export const Raw: new (...args: any[]) => Knex.Raw;
```

Usage:

```ts
import { Raw, QueryBuilderClass } from 'knex';

function isKnexQuery(value: unknown): value is Knex.Raw | Knex.QueryBuilder {
  return value instanceof QueryBuilderClass || value instanceof Raw;
}
```

(`Raw` is free today, so it can take the natural name; only the builder needs a non-colliding one. Naming is obviously up for discussion — `QueryBuilderClass`, `QueryBuilderConstructor`, or namespacing both under something like `knex.classes.*` all work for me.)

**Option B — ship a first-class type guard instead.** If exposing the constructors is not desirable (see #6269 — `instanceof` is unreliable when two copies of knex end up in the same dependency tree, which is exactly the failure mode the classes would reintroduce for consumers), then a guard exported by knex itself would solve the use case more robustly, since knex can implement it with whatever internal marker it prefers:

```ts
import { isKnexQuery, isRaw, isQueryBuilder } from 'knex';

if (isKnexQuery(value)) { /* value: Knex.Raw | Knex.QueryBuilder */ }
```

Option B is arguably the better long-term answer and composes well with the `Symbol.for()` proposal in #6269; Option A is the smaller change. Either one removes the need for a deep import into `knex/lib/*`.

Happy to open a PR for whichever direction the maintainers prefer.
